            <ArticleTitle>The incidence of dorsal radiocarpal ligament tears in patients having diagnostic wrist arthroscopy for wrist pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>332-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jhsa.2007.11.026</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The goal of this study was to examine the incidence of dorsal radiocarpal ligament (DRCL) tears in patients having diagnostic arthroscopy for chronic wrist pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A chart review was performed of 64 patients who had diagnostic wrist arthroscopy for chronic wrist pain that was refractory to conservative measures. For each case, interosseous ligament instability/tears were graded according to the Geissler classification. Tears of the triangular fibrocartilage complex and the presence or absence of a DRCL tear were noted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 35 of 64 wrists (in 64 patients) with DRCL tears. The average duration of wrist pain prior to treatment was 20 months. Only 10 patients could recall a specific injury. Five patients had an isolated DRCL tear. A scapholunate interosseous ligament injury was identified in 13 patients, of whom 7 had a concomitant DRCL tear. A lunotriquetral interosseous ligament injury was present in 7 patients, of whom 2 had a concomitant DRCL tear. Two patients had a capitohamate ligament tear: 1 of these patients had a DRCL tear. There were 7 patients with a solitary triangular fibrocartilage complex tear: 6 of 7 were in association with a DRCL tear. One patient had a chronic ulnar styloid nonunion and a DRCL tear. Two or more lesions were present in 23 patients; DRCL tears were present in 12.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">DRCL tears are commonly seen with injuries to the primary wrist stabilizers. Recognition of this condition and further research into treatment methods are needed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TYPE OF STUDY/LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Diagnostic IV.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
